WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.049
Speaker 1: The midterm election season for
2022 is likely to start

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.430
Speaker 2: earlier than ever this
Speaker 1: time around.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:12.000
I was talking to
Speaker 2: our YouTube rep recently, and he

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.500
said, expect interest in these
Speaker 1: 2022 midterms to start and to see

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.360
more serious
Speaker 2: campaigning and interest on

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.660
Speaker 1: YouTube as early as spring of next
year, rather than more like early summer.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.230
And there are some really good reasons why
we would expect that not only are there increasingly

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:37.750
interesting particular races to look at, including
the Texas gubernatorial election where Beto

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:43.290
O'Rourke is running Pennsylvania Senate, we
now have this quack cure promoter Dr. Mehmet

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.290
Oz.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.620
Speaker 2: We also have John
Speaker 1: Fetterman running on the Democratic

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.620
side.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:48.620
OK, that's interesting.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.960
So we've got interesting races starting to
fill the

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.030
Speaker 2: docket,
Speaker 1: but the stakes are extremely high

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:59.969
in terms of who controls the House and Senate
starting in January of 2023.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:04.519
And we could go through a long list of reasons
that this is important, including to what

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.750
degree Republicans will be able to
Speaker 2: stonewall anything

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.450
Speaker 1: Joe Biden tries to do.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.600
They've they've been partially stonewalling
everything so far.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.360
Speaker 2: They might completely
Speaker 1: stonewall everything if they take

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.030
control of the house.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:24.320
But maybe just as importantly, is the reality
that the current Republican Party is wackier

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.020
than it has ever been.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:32.130
And among their goals is this simmering idea
of making Donald Trump speaker of the House.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.580
If they take control of the House of Representatives
in November.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.439
Now you might be saying, Whoa, whoa, stop
the clock.

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:40.840
Speaker 2: David Trump isn't
Speaker 1: running for a house.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.520
How could it be the speaker you may or may
not know?

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.650
One does not have to be a member of the House
of Representatives to be speaker of the House,

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:54.390
where I'll talk to Ro Khanna about this when
we have him back on the show soon.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:55.790
The House can vote for anybody.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:02:00.860
If the House decides they want me to be speaker
of the House, I could be speaker of the House.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.690
And starting months ago,
Speaker 2: there was this idea which was mentioned

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:09.399
Speaker 1: on Steve Bannon's bizarre program
of installing Donald Trump as speaker of the

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:13.299
House, even without him being a member of
the House of Representatives, impeaching Joe

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:19.719
Biden and then having Donald Trump preside
over the vote to

00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:23.180
Speaker 2: impeach Joe
Speaker 1: Biden in the House beleaguered

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.540
radical Republican Congressman
Speaker 2: Matt Gaetz is really

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.370
Speaker 1: into this idea.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.829
Starting back in July, he started talking
about

00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:34.990
Speaker 2: If we win in twenty two,
Speaker 1: we will make Donald Trump Speaker

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:35.990
of the House.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.900
Speaker 2: And at the
Speaker 1: time, it seemed that it might have

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.949
just been a publicity ploy
Speaker 2: to fundraise, get attention,

00:02:41.949 --> 00:02:44.709
Speaker 1: maybe raise money for his legal
defense fund.

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:47.749
But Matt Gaetz mentioned it again in August.

00:02:47.749 --> 00:02:49.340
And he mentioned it again yesterday.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:54.169
Senior White House correspondent for Yahoo
News Alexander Nazarian published a tweet

00:02:54.169 --> 00:03:00.400
about this, saying quote Matt Gaetz just told
me at a press conference that if Republicans

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:07.049
win the House in twenty two, he will move
to install Trump as House speaker.

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:09.919
So there are really three questions here.

00:03:09.919 --> 00:03:12.400
Question one Is Matt Gaetz serious?

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.819
He appears to be.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:15.409
Second question.

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:21.090
Would Republicans actually nominate Trump
to be speaker of the House if they took control?

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.139
Speaker 2: And the third question is, if
Speaker 1: nominated, would

00:03:24.139 --> 00:03:27.790
Speaker 2: Republicans overall
Speaker 1: actually vote to make Donald Trump

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:28.790
speaker of the House?

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.669
The answers to the first two questions are
pretty simple.

00:03:30.669 --> 00:03:34.260
Matt Gaetz is serious,
Speaker 2: and I do think that Donald Trump

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.979
would be
Speaker 1: nominated for speaker of the House

00:03:35.979 --> 00:03:38.750
if Republicans take control in 2022.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:44.559
The third question is the key question just
because Republicans take control and someone

00:03:44.559 --> 00:03:47.040
nominates Trump to be speaker.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.419
Do they really elect Trump to be speaker
Speaker 2: of the House?

00:03:49.419 --> 00:03:55.230
Speaker 1: Understand that it's an open question,
not morally, but strategically.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.339
If Trump is don't do particularly well in
the November midterms.

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:04.840
If Donald Trump's power within the party seems
diminished, it's less likely that Republicans

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.909
would be willing
Speaker 2: to do something so cartoonish.

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:10.450
Speaker 1: On the other hand, and particularly
if Republicans take control of the House

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:14.290
Speaker 2: on the basis of many
Speaker 1: Trumpists winning new seats in

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:17.419
the House, it is completely
Speaker 2: plausible

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:20.680
Speaker 1: that Republicans would
Speaker 2: be willing to make Trump

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:25.210
Speaker 1: speaker of the House, and it would
be one of the most cartoonish and pathetic

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:29.970
circumstances in American politics in a very,
very long time.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:35.740
Unfortunately, the combination of historical
trends where the first midterm after a new

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.270
president is usually bad for that party, meaning
it would be bad for Democrats in November

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.270
combined.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:45.930
The gerrymandering comment, combined with
population shifts that might see Democrats

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.840
losing a few seats just on that basis.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.590
Speaker 2: It is looking
Speaker 1: more and more like Republicans

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.110
are going to win the House in November.

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:57.020
It's not a
Speaker 2: guarantee, and when I

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:00.370
Speaker 1: say that, I'm not saying it so
that we do nothing.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:05.199
I'm saying it so that we don't throw in the
towel and we actually fight for the house

00:05:05.199 --> 00:05:08.242
because if we lose that house, it's going
to be very, very bad for a long period of

00:05:08.242 --> 00:05:08.250
time.

